3	// Regression coverage for source networks whose prefix length does not fall
4	// on an IPv4 octet boundary, where a mask computed against the wrong octet
5	// silently widens or narrows the admitted range:
6	//
7	//   - 172.16.5.9/12 admits exactly 172.16.0.0 - 172.31.255.255 (the boundary
8	//     sits inside the second octet): both range ends are admitted, the
9	//     addresses immediately before and after the range are not.
10	//   - 192.0.129.7/17 admits exactly 192.0.128.0 - 192.0.255.255 (the boundary
11	//     sits inside the third octet), with the same edge behavior.
12	//   - 192.0.2.3/31 is the two-address network 192.0.2.2 - 192.0.2.3: both
13	//     addresses take part in filtering — neither is set aside as a network or
14	//     broadcast address — while 192.0.2.1 and 192.0.2.4 stay out.
15	//
16	// Spelling the same network with a different in-range host address changes
17	// nothing, a boundary source arrives through source_ip or src_ip
18	// interchangeably, and a plain IPv4 address and its IPv4-mapped IPv6 spelling
19	// share one decision and one dotted output form, while a genuine IPv6 source
20	// with the same trailing 32 bits never matches. Dropped lines and blank lines
21	// never renumber later records, an out-of-range line with an invalid
22	// timestamp is still a counted failure with its reason and no event, and a
23	// batch in which every valid log misses the network ends cleanly with empty
24	// output and zero failures.
